# SUPERSEDED. The conclusion this file was written to record is WRONG.
#
# Superseded by: evidence/20260712-hw023-es8311-reset-is-innocent-adc-needs-6s.log
#
# The captured serial output below is untouched and is still a true record of what the
# hardware did. It is the interpretation that was wrong, and it was wrong in a way this
# run could not have detected.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE ORIGINALLY CLAIMED
# ---------------------------------
#   "HW-019 diagnostic - the software reset deafens the ADC below ~22 kHz."
#   "Every register the driver writes reads back correctly. The ADC is deaf anyway."
#   "Root cause NOT established. The reset is not shipped; see the binding."
#
# WHY IT IS WRONG
# ---------------
# The rate sweep below walks 8000, 11025, 12000, 16000, 22050, 24000, 32000, 44100,
# 48000 -- always, and only, in that order. That is increasing sample rate. It is also,
# unavoidably, increasing time since the reset, at roughly two seconds a rate.
#
# So "deaf below 22 kHz" and "deaf for the first ten seconds" fit these same nine numbers
# equally well, and nothing in this run, or in any run of this sweep ever made, could
# tell them apart.
#
# Reversing the rate table separates them, and there is no third outcome. With the same
# reset, 48 kHz measured FIRST came back deaf (tone_rms=1) and 8 kHz measured LAST came
# back alive (tone_rms=923, against a no-reset baseline of 777). The deafness follows the
# clock. It does not follow the rate. There is no low-sample-rate cutoff and there never
# was one.
#
# WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE
# ---------------------
# The ES8311's analog reference sits on three 1 uF capacitors -- VMID, ADCVREF, DACVREF;
# the part has no microphone-bias pin at all. The 0x00 block reset clears CSM_ON, which
# powers the analog down and discharges them. Charging them back takes about 6.2 seconds,
# and the ADC returns a zero noise floor until it is done. Then it works normally, and it
# keeps working. Measured directly, at one fixed rate, one configure, one START:
#
#     t=543..3103 ms   floor=0
#     t=5663 ms        floor=35
#     t=6175 ms        floor=101      <- awake; the settled floor here is 81..116
#     t=9759 ms        floor=113
#
# The conclusion "do not reset this part" survives. Every reason given for it here does
# not. And the driver does not need a reset anyway: writing every register it depends on
# reaches the same known state at zero settling cost.
#
# ================================================================================
